    worked:[
      { lbl:'Acknowledge, set the boundary kindly', txt:'"I really want to help, and I\u2019m sorry you\u2019re feeling rough \u2014 but I can\u2019t prescribe \u2018off the record\u2019 or without a proper look, even for a colleague. That\u2019s not me being awkward; it\u2019s about keeping you safe and doing this properly, and it\u2019s the same standard I\u2019d hold for anyone."' },
      { lbl:'Treat her as any patient', txt:'"What I can do is see you properly — a quick proper consultation — check your temperature, breathing rate and oxygen, examine your chest, and prescribe if it’s genuinely needed, all documented as it should be. Most chest infections like this are viral and settle without antibiotics, so antibiotics only if you actually need them, same as any patient."' },
      { lbl:'Reassure on confidentiality', txt:'"On privacy — your records are confidential, and colleagues here can’t just look at them; there are access controls, access is audited, and I can ask for your record to be restricted. Confidentiality is the point, but that means proper, protected records, not no records at all."' },
      { lbl:'Encourage own GP', txt:'"Honestly, for things like your sleep \u2014 and anything ongoing or personal \u2014 it\u2019s much better to be registered with your own GP elsewhere, so you\u2019re not getting care at your own workplace. I\u2019d really encourage that."' },
      { lbl:'Explore underlying', txt:'"Can I gently ask — the not sleeping and feeling run down, how are things generally, work and stress-wise? How has your mood been — any times you’ve felt hopeless or thought of harming yourself? And how much are you drinking? I’d rather understand that than just hand over sleeping tablets, which are controlled drugs, aren’t usually the answer and can cause dependence."' },
      { lbl:'Plan', txt:'"So let’s book you a proper slot — with me now if you like, or your own GP — sort the chest properly, and talk about your sleep and stress the right way. If you need a few days off, you can self-certify for up to a week. That’s the best care I can give you."' },
    ],
    learning:'A colleague or member of staff requesting INFORMAL, "off-the-record", unrecorded treatment raises clear PROFESSIONALISM issues. GMC Good medical practice (2024) is to avoid, wherever possible, providing medical care to people with whom you have a close personal relationship (a colleague is not automatically one), and GMC prescribing guidance (2021) is never to prescribe without an adequate assessment or without keeping adequate RECORDS \u2014 "corridor consultations" and off-the-record prescriptions are not acceptable. Treat a colleague as you would any patient: a proper consultation (history/examination as needed), the same prescribing standards and stewardship (antibiotics only if indicated — NICE NG120 acute cough, NICE NG250 if pneumonia suspected), and full documentation in their records \u2014 do not cut corners or omit records because they are staff, as this compromises patient safety and governance. Maintain appropriate confidentiality and boundaries: the colleague is entitled to the same confidentiality as any patient (and practice colleagues should not access their records without legitimate cause \u2014 reassure them about access controls), but confidentiality means proper, access-controlled records, not hidden or unrecorded care, and the dual relationship (the GP may also be her employer) must be managed sensitively. Explain kindly why informal/unrecorded prescribing is not possible \u2014 patient safety, governance, fairness and professional duties \u2014 without being dismissive, and offer a proper alternative: book a proper appointment and, ideally, encourage the colleague to be registered with and see their OWN GP, particularly for ongoing or sensitive issues. Be alert to underlying issues — stress/burnout, low mood and self-harm (ask directly), alcohol, and especially requests for sleeping tablets (zopiclone and zolpidem are Schedule 4 Part 1 controlled drugs; NICE TA77 short-term use only; NICE NG215 dependence) or signs of dependence/self-treatment — and address these supportively; she can self-certify for up to 7 days if she needs time off. The skill is upholding professional boundaries (no informal/unrecorded prescribing), explaining kindly, ensuring proper care and confidentiality, and signposting appropriate registration/care. No NICE NG12 (updated April 2026) cancer pathway applies.',
