    worked:[
      { lbl:'Recognise the cluster', txt:'"Those symptoms together \u2014 constant hunger and thirst, weeing a lot, losing weight despite eating, and exhaustion \u2014 strongly suggest your blood sugar is high; in other words, diabetes. Let me check it right now with a finger-prick."' },
      { lbl:'Exclude DKA', txt:'"A few important checks \u2014 any vomiting, tummy pain, drowsiness, or fast deep breathing? \u2026 No, and you\u2019re not dehydrated. I\u2019ll also check for ketones, because if your sugar is very high with ketones you can become seriously unwell and need same-day hospital care."' },
      { lbl:'Confirm', txt:'"Your glucose is high. I’ll confirm with a laboratory glucose test — the usual HbA1c test can under-read when symptoms have only been there a few weeks — and given your symptoms that’s usually enough to diagnose diabetes. The pattern fits type 2, but because you’re under 50 and losing weight I’ll keep the type that needs insulin in mind."' },
      { lbl:'Differentiate if needed', txt:'"If anything didn\u2019t fit, I\u2019d also check your thyroid and look for other causes of weight loss \u2014 but the thirst and weeing point clearly to diabetes here."' },
      { lbl:'Initiate management', txt:'"For type 2, we start with diet and lifestyle and usually a slow-release tablet called metformin, with a second tablet called an SGLT2 inhibitor added later, look after your heart risk, arrange eye, foot and kidney checks, and get you proper diabetes education. With those tablets you don’t need to tell the DVLA for a normal car licence. We’ll do this step by step."' },
      { lbl:'Safety-net', txt:'"If you become very unwell \u2014 vomiting, drowsy, breathing fast, severe tummy pain \u2014 that’s an emergency, call 999 or go straight to A&E. Otherwise we\u2019ll get your tests done and start treatment."' },
    ],
    learning:'POLYPHAGIA (increased appetite/constant hunger) with the OSMOTIC symptoms of hyperglycaemia \u2014 POLYDIPSIA (thirst), POLYURIA (excess urination), unexplained WEIGHT LOSS despite eating, and fatigue \u2014 is the classic cluster of new DIABETES, commonly TYPE 2 in an overweight, middle-aged patient with family history, though TYPE 1/LADA must be considered — NICE NG17 features are ketosis, rapid weight loss, age under 50, BMI below 25 and autoimmune history, and age or BMI alone must not exclude it. Critically, screen for and exclude acute decompensation/DIABETIC KETOACIDOSIS \u2014 being very unwell, vomiting, abdominal pain, drowsiness/confusion, deep rapid (Kussmaul) breathing, ketotic ("pear-drop") breath and dehydration \u2014 which is a medical emergency needing same-day assessment/admission (especially if type 1 is suspected), so check capillary glucose AND ketones. Confirm the diagnosis appropriately (capillary glucose and ketones now; laboratory fasting plasma glucose ≥7.0 / random ≥11.1 with symptoms; HbA1c ≥48 mmol/mol only where appropriate), noting that in a symptomatic patient one diagnostic test suffices and that WHO 2011 (international) advises against HbA1c for diagnosis when symptoms are under two months or type 1 is suspected (use plasma glucose). Where the picture is atypical or glucose normal, differentiate other causes of polyphagia with weight loss \u2014 hyperthyroidism (TFTs), malignancy, malabsorption \u2014 but the osmotic symptoms strongly point to diabetes. For confirmed type 2 diabetes, initiate management per NICE NG28 (updated February 2026: lifestyle, modified-release metformin then an SGLT2 inhibitor with DKA counselling, renal considerations, structured education), cardiovascular-risk management (NICE NG238 atorvastatin 20 mg if QRISK 10% or more), complication screening — retinal, foot, renal/ACR — and DVLA advice, and refer urgently/admit if DKA or type 1 is suspected. Safety-net for deterioration. NICE NG12 (updated April 2026): new-onset diabetes with weight loss at 60 or over prompts urgent pancreatic imaging — not applicable at 48, but reassess weight loss that continues after glucose control.',
    knowledge:{
      guideline:'WHO diabetes criteria (2006) and HbA1c (2011) (international) · NICE NG28 Type 2 diabetes in adults (updated February 2026) · NICE NG17 Type 1 diabetes in adults · JBDS DKA (2023) · NICE NG238 CVD risk (2023) · NICE NG12 (updated April 2026) · DVLA diabetes · MHRA DSU March 2020 SGLT2 inhibitors · new diabetes: exclude DKA · confirm with plasma glucose · type 1 vs 2 · initiate type 2 management',
      points:[
        { h:'Recognise', t:'Polyphagia + polydipsia + polyuria + weight loss + fatigue = new diabetes. Type 2 (overweight/FH) vs type 1/LADA (NICE NG17: ketosis, rapid weight loss, under 50, BMI <25, autoimmune history; not age/BMI alone).' },
        { h:'Exclude DKA', t:'Vomiting, abdominal pain, drowsiness, Kussmaul breathing, ketotic breath, dehydration \u2192 same-day emergency. Check glucose AND ketones.' },
        { h:'Confirm', t:'Capillary glucose + ketones now; lab fasting ≥7.0/random ≥11.1 with symptoms. HbA1c ≥48 not for diagnosis if symptoms under 2 months or type 1 suspected (WHO 2011) — use plasma glucose.' },
        { h:'Differentiate', t:'If atypical/normal glucose: hyperthyroidism (TFTs), malignancy, malabsorption. Osmotic symptoms point to diabetes.' },
        { h:'Initiate type 2 mgmt', t:'Lifestyle, MR metformin then SGLT2 inhibitor (NICE NG28 Feb 2026; renal considerations; DKA counselling), CV risk (NICE NG238), structured education, complication screening (eyes/feet/renal/ACR), DVLA advice.' },
        { h:'Refer urgently if', t:'DKA (JBDS 2023), raised ketones or type 1 suspected → urgent same-day diabetes/medical assessment, insulin.' },
        { h:'Never do', t:'Never miss DKA/type-1 features; never delay if unwell; never rely on HbA1c in rapid-onset type 1; never overlook complication screening.' },
        { h:'Safety-net & follow-up', t:'Same-day/urgent assessment for DKA/type-1; confirm diagnosis + initiate management; complication screening; review; emergency advice for deterioration.' }
      ]
    }
